  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAfter some early morning fog, today should be sunny. Highs will\nbe around 60 far interior and near 70 across the lower\nelevations of NH and the ME coastal plain.\n\nClear and calm tonight with lows in the 30s to lower 40s. At\nthis time it does not look like it will get cold enough to\nrequire frost headlines where the program is still active.\n\nHigh pressure exits the region on Sunday as an upper level \ntrough swings in from the Great Lakes Region. Clouds are going \nto build in early which is going to keep afternoon temperatures \nin the low to mid-60s. These clouds are also going to make for a\nmore mild night as temperatures only fall into the low to mid \n40s.\n\nA strong cold front crosses on Monday and it continues to look \nlike it is going to be mostly dry except for a few showers,\nespecially in the north. The bigger theme will be wind as the\npressure gradient tightens markedly with and behind the front\nfor Monday afternoon. Forecast soundings support gusts of 25 to\n35 MPH, possible higher if low level mixing is maximized.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nThe real cool down arrives by Tuesday morning. Very likely the \ncoldest weather since the spring Tuesday-Wednesday with 850 mb \ntemperatures plunging to between 0 and -7C by Tuesday morning \ndepending on model of choice. Even 925 mb temperatures are near or \nslightly below freezing on guidance. As such, even modest \nradiational cooling would suggest a widespread freeze Tuesday \nmorning. However this is contingent on the boundary layer \ndecoupling, and guidance is currently indicating 40-50+ kts at 850 \nmb driven by a strong pressure gradient, so would say the \ntemperature forecast here is not yet high confidence. Right now the \nbest odds for below freezing minimums will be in the higher terrain \nin the interior, with lows in the upper 30s-low 40s closer to the \ncoastal plain.\n\nWon't take much mixing on Tuesday to tap the stronger winds aloft, \nso can expect a blustery day with sustained winds of 10-20 mph \nthrough the day. Even with full mixing highs will only reach the 40s \nin the mountains with 55-60 on the coastal plain. Winter clothing \nmay not be a bad choice especially if heading out early in the day.\n\nDry and sunny conditions should last through Wednesday with high \npressure cresting overhead. While 850 mb temps won't be quite as \ncold Wed morning, much greater radiational cooling is likely as winds \nslack. It is likely a larger swath of western ME and \nsouthern/central NH see their first hard freeze of the fall, \nespecially in any valleys/typical cold spots away from the immediate \ncoast.\n\nFair weather should continue Wednesday with highs primarily in the \nmid 50s-low 60s. The next chance for precipitation does not arrive \nuntil Wednesday night-Thursday.",
